@*
    The shared card a per-selection surface renders when the selection is a
    change-history view: its rows are serialized history records that back the
    revision timeline rather than directly inspectable value data, so every
    surface routes the operator to the source table with one consistent message
    and one consistent action.

    It lives in the kernel because two independent plugin packages render it
    (the value drill-down and the live-metrics surfaces) and neither may
    reference the other. Navigation is a callback rather than an injected
    selection service, so the card itself reaches nothing.
*@
<div class="lx-selection-guidance">
    <p class="lx-selection-guidance-title">This is a change-history view.</p>
    <p class="lx-selection-guidance-body">
        Its rows are serialized history records that back the
        <strong>History</strong> timeline, not directly inspectable value
        data. Inspect its timeline from its source table
        @if (Selection.SourceTreeId is { Length: > 0 } named)
        {
            <code>@named</code>
        }
        else
        {
            <span>(unknown)</span>
        }:
        select the source table, open a key in its <strong>Data</strong>
        surface, then choose <strong>History</strong>.
    </p>
    @if (Selection.SourceTreeId is { Length: > 0 } sourceTreeId)
    {
        <button type="button"
                class="lx-btn lx-selection-guidance-action"
                @onclick="@(() => OnGoToSourceTree.InvokeAsync(sourceTreeId))">
            Go to source table @sourceTreeId
        </button>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The selected change-history view this guidance describes.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public CatalogItem Selection { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// Invoked with the source tree id when the operator asks to go there. The
    /// hosting surface performs the navigation through its own declared domain
    /// contract, so this card needs no selection service of its own.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnGoToSourceTree { get; set; }
}
